# Airbnb Price Prediction

## Project Overview

This project develops an end-to-end machine learning system to predict the nightly price of Airbnb listings in New York City.

The project covers data analysis, data preprocessing, feature engineering, regression model comparison, hyperparameter tuning, model evaluation, and deployment through a Streamlit web application.

## Dataset

Dataset: New York City Airbnb Open Data (AB_NYC_2019.csv)

The dataset contains Airbnb listing information including:

- Location
- Room type
- Price
- Minimum nights
- Number of reviews
- Reviews per month
- Host listing count
- Availability

## Project Workflow

The project follows the following workflow:

1. Data loading and exploration
2. Missing value analysis
3. Duplicate and invalid-value checks
4. Outlier analysis
5. Data cleaning and preprocessing
6. Feature engineering
7. Exploratory data analysis
8. Train-test split
9. Regression model training
10. Model comparison
11. Random Forest hyperparameter tuning
12. Final model evaluation
13. Feature importance analysis
14. Streamlit application development

## Data Preprocessing

The following preprocessing steps were performed:

- Listings with zero price were removed.
- Missing `reviews_per_month` values were replaced with 0 because they corresponded to listings with zero reviews.
- `last_review` was removed from modelling.
- Missing categorical values were handled during preprocessing.
- Numerical features were standardized using StandardScaler.
- Categorical features were encoded using OneHotEncoder.
- Log transformations were applied to selected skewed numerical variables.
- The target price was log-transformed for modelling.

## Models Evaluated

The following regression models were compared:

| Model | MAE | RMSE | R² |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| Linear Regression | 0.3375 | 0.4695 | 0.5410 |
| Ridge Regression | 0.3375 | 0.4692 | 0.5415 |
| Random Forest | 0.3147 | 0.4476 | 0.5829 |
| Gradient Boosting | 0.3243 | 0.4536 | 0.5715 |

Random Forest achieved the best initial performance based on the comparison metrics.

## Hyperparameter Tuning

Randomized Search was used to tune the Random Forest model.

The tuned Random Forest was selected as the final model.

### Final Model Performance

- MAE: $55.28
- RMSE: $180.17
- R²: 0.1892

## Important Features

The most important features identified by the Random Forest model included:

- Room type
- Longitude
- Latitude
- Availability
- Minimum nights
- Reviews per month
- Host listing count
- Neighbourhood group
- Number of reviews
- Neighbourhood

## Streamlit Application

A Streamlit web application was developed to allow users to enter Airbnb listing information and receive an estimated nightly price.

The application accepts information such as:

- Latitude
- Longitude
- Minimum nights
- Number of reviews
- Reviews per month
- Host listing count
- Availability
- Neighbourhood group
- Neighbourhood
- Room type

## Application Testing

The application was tested using realistic listing inputs.

| Test | Location | Room Type | Predicted Price |
|---|---|---|---:|
| 1 | Manhattan / Midtown | Entire home/apt | $279.90 |
| 2 | Brooklyn / Williamsburg | Private room | $81.61 |
| 3 | Queens / Long Island City | Private room | $76.11 |

## Limitations

The model has several limitations:

- Airbnb prices can vary due to factors that are not present in the dataset.
- The model's predictive performance is limited, as indicated by the final R² value.
- Location and room type have a strong influence on predictions.
- The dataset represents a historical snapshot and may not reflect current Airbnb pricing.
- Extreme and unusual listings can make price prediction difficult.




